In JavaScript, keywords and identifiers are both important for writing programs, but they have different purposes.

1. Keywords

Keywords are reserved words in JavaScript that have a special meaning to the language. You cannot normally use them as variable, function, or class names.

Common JavaScript keywords:

Keyword	Purpose	Example
let	Declare a variable	let age = 25;
const	Declare a constant	const pi = 3.14;
var	Declare a variable	var name = "John";
if	Conditional statement	if (age > 18)
else	Alternative condition	else { ... }
for	Loop	for (...)
while	Loop	while (...)
function	Define a function	function login() {}
return	Return a value	return result;
class	Define a class	class User {}
new	Create an object	new User()
this	Refers to current object/context	this.name
try	Start exception handling	try { ... }
catch	Handle an error	catch (error) {}
throw	Throw an error	throw new Error()
async	Define async function	async function test()
await	Wait for a Promise	await page.goto()

Example:

let age = 25;

if (age >= 18) {
    return true;
}

Here:

let, if, and return → keywords
age → identifier
2. Identifiers

Identifiers are names that you give to variables, functions, classes, objects, etc.

Example:

let userName = "Anas";
let age = 25;

function login() {
    console.log("Login successful");
}

Here:

userName → identifier
age → identifier
login → identifier

Identifiers are created by the programmer.
